[staging/src/k8s.io/api/resource/v1/types.go] ```suggestion
	//   - Checking domain existence first (still assumes that if the domain exists, it always has the  field):
```

[staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/cel/errors.go] ```suggestion
		return fmt.Errorf("%w. Consider using CEL optional chaining (.?), has() macro, or orValue() for optional fields", err)
```

[staging/src/k8s.io/api/resource/v1/types.go] Good point! The CEL Optional Types link is indeed specifically about handling "no such key" errors, so it makes more sense to group it with that bullet item.

```go
I'll restructure it to:
  // A robust expression should check for the existence of attributes
  // before referencing them.
  //
  // Common errors:
  // - "no such key": Use has() macro, optional chaining (.?), or orValue()
  //   for optional fields. See CEL Optional Types for details:
  //   https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/google/cel-go@v0.17.4/cel#OptionalTypes
  //
  // For more CEL expression syntax and examples, see:
  // https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/cel/
```

This should make the documentation clearer and more concise.

[staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/cel/errors.go] You're right, this is a valid concern for a public library function that may be called in multiple places.
I plan to use a custom error type with errors.As for idempotency, similar to how other error enhancement functions in Kubernetes work (they check specific error types and return new types, making them naturally idempotent):
```go
type enhancedError struct {
    error
}
func (e *enhancedError) Unwrap() error { return e.error }
func EnhanceRuntimeError(err error) error {
    if err == nil {
        return nil
    }
    
    var enhanced *enhancedError
    if errors.As(err, &enhanced) {
        return err
    }
    
    if strings.Contains(err.Error(), "no such key:") {
        return &enhancedError{
            error: fmt.Errorf("%w. consider using CEL optional chaining (...)", err),
        }
    }
    return err
}
```

