human_review:
[staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/registry/rest/validate.go] How about folding it into the `DeclarativeValidationStrategy` interface and giving `rest.DeclarativeValidation` struct a default empty implementation. Currently,  a strategy can implement
  `DeclarativeValidationConfig(...)` (returning Options, NormalizationRules, etc.) but forget
  to embed rest.DeclarativeValidation.

e.g.
```go
 // DeclarativeValidationStrategy defines how a strategy may opt-in to declarative validation.
  type DeclarativeValidationStrategy interface {
      ValidateDeclaratively(ctx context.Context, obj, oldObj runtime.Object, validationErrs
  field.ErrorList, opType operation.Type, config DeclarativeValidationConfig) field.ErrorList

      // DeclarativeValidationConfig is called per request to configure declarative validation.
      // Strategies that don't need configuration can rely on the default (empty) implementation
      // provided by rest.DeclarativeValidation.
      DeclarativeValidationConfig(ctx context.Context, obj, oldObj runtime.Object)
  DeclarativeValidationConfig
  }

  type DeclarativeValidation struct {
      *runtime.Scheme
  }

  func (d DeclarativeValidation) ValidateDeclaratively(ctx context.Context, obj, oldObj runtime.Object,
  validationErrs field.ErrorList, opType operation.Type, config DeclarativeValidationConfig)
  field.ErrorList {
      if d.Scheme == nil {
          return append(validationErrs, field.InternalError(nil, fmt.Errorf("cannot validate declaratively
  without a scheme")))
      }
      return ValidateDeclarativelyWithMigrationChecks(ctx, d.Scheme, obj, oldObj, validationErrs, opType,
  config)
  }

  // Default empty config — strategies override to supply Options/NormalizationRules/etc.
  func (DeclarativeValidation) DeclarativeValidationConfig(ctx context.Context, obj, oldObj runtime.Object)
   DeclarativeValidationConfig {
      return DeclarativeValidationConfig{}
  }
```

[pkg/registry/storage/volumeattachment/strategy.go] I find the convention of embedding `ObjectTyper` in each strategy and initializing it with `legacyscheme.Scheme` not particularly readable (as an assurance interface got implemented).  We don't use that idiom much elsewhere (type assertions are a lot more common).

Here, I'm instead doing:

```go
type DeclarativeValidation struct {
	*runtime.Scheme
    // ...
}
```

Which impelements `ObjecTyper`.

I suppose I could add type assertions to make this more obvious, but there is a lot of duck typing that we don't call out explicitly, and I'm not convinced highlighting `ObjecTyper` explicitly is all that helpful to the average reader.

