[pkg/controller/scheduling/podgroupprotection/podgroup_protection_controller.go] For Job controller workflow, the Job controller creates PodGroup first, then creates Pods sequentially. So the PodGroup creation event reaches the informer cache well before any Pod is created.

In the delete case, the PodGroup would typically be user-initiated, arriving long after both PodGroup and Pod events have been processed by the informer.

The scenario you shared (very valid race condition) requires a very tight window when PodGroup is created, Pod is created, PodGroup is deleted .. all before the Pod create event reaches the informer cache.

The PVC protection controller accepted this same trade-off after #125372, switching from live lookups to informer-based lookups for performance.

That said, there's a practical mitigation that avoids live lookups by a requeue-before-remove pattern. Instead of immediately removing the finalizer when `isBeingUsed` returns false, requeue the PodGroup with a short delay. On the second pass, if still no active pods reference it, then remove the finalizer. This gives the Pod informer time to catch up. Something like:

```go
if protectionutil.IsDeletionCandidate(pg, PodGroupProtectionFinalizer) {
    isUsed, err := c.isBeingUsed(ctx, pg)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    if !isUsed {
        // Requeue once with a delay to let the pod informer catch up
        // before committing to finalizer removal.
        if !c.hasBeenRequeued(pgKey) {
            c.markRequeued(pgKey)
            c.queue.AddAfter(pgKey, 5*time.Second)
            return nil
        }
        c.clearRequeued(pgKey)
        return c.removeFinalizer(ctx, pg)
    }
}
```

The requeued state can be a simple `sync.Map` to track which PodGroup keys have already been given a grace period.

There is an alternative solution by adding the finalizer unconditionally at PodGroup creation time either by the Job controller when it creates the PodGroup, or via a mutating admission webhook. That way the finalizer exists before any pods are created. The protection controller would then only be responsible for removal.

