[pkg/controller/job/job_scheduling_manager.go] This isn't correct. The fact that workload exists doesn't mean that PodGroup should also exist.

As an example, the controller might have terminated/crashed whatever after creating Workload but before creating the PodGroup.

The sequence should really be:
```
discoverWorkload()
if workload == nil {
  if len(pods) > 0 { return }
   ensureWorkload()
}

discoverPodGroup()
if podGRoup == nil {
  ensurePodGRoup()
}
```

[pkg/controller/job/job_controller.go] The logic here seems fine, but I'd suggest simplifying the inline comment. We generally care about the declarative state rather than who or what created it.

It doesn't necessarily have to be a "higher-level controller" — it could simply be a user explicitly defining their own schedulingGroup. Let's just focus on the behavior: if it's already set, we respect it; if not, we inject the one managed by this controller.

```
// If the template already has schedulingGroup set, we respect the existing 
