[staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/cel/compile.go] Suppose some ResourceSlice got stored with list values while the feature was enabled. Then the feature gets disabled and the scheduler evaluates a CEL expression against it. What should happen?

The cluster is basically in a degraded state. The goal has to be to not make the situation worse. I think that means that evaluation the CEL expression and therefore scheduling should fail. The fix for the cluster then is to recreate the ResourceSlices without the list values.

In practice, that is exactly what happens because we fall through to `return nil, errors.New("unsupported attribute value")` below.

However, it isn't clear that this was intentional. Let's add a source code comment above this if check:
```
// If the feature is disabled and an attribute
// contains a list value, then we fall through
// to returning the "unsupported attribute value" error
// below. This failure then aborts scheduling
// until ResourceSlices get updated. This
// is better than incorrectly scheduling a pod
// because that is harder to correct.
```

[pkg/apis/resource/validation/validation.go] This approach means that if someone sends `{"bools": [], "int": 1}`, they only get told that "bools" must not be empty. They don't get the "exactly one value must be specified" error. Then if they fix the non-emptiness, they still have a problem.

I prefer:
```
if attribute.BoolValues != nil {
    numFields++
    if len(attribute.BoolValues) == 0 {
           allErrs = append(allErrs, field.Invalid(fldPath.Child("bools"), attribute.BoolValues, "must not be empty if specified"))
    }
}
```

[staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/cel/compile_test.go] > Actually, does it use 64 * 64 for string operations that iterate over all characters in a string list? Worth a test case, even if it is a bit contrived.

Although I'm not a CEL expert. It seems not. cel-go defines [`StringTraversalCostFactor(=0.1)`](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/aa593eb4fefec68b123552fa7283da6effedad06/vendor/github.com/google/cel-go/common/cost.go#L33-L35). So, in this case, the estimate would be `64(items) * 6.4(equality check for 64 length string)`.

For references, `StringTraversalCostFactor` is used in cost estimators:
- [cel-go' cost estimator](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/aa593eb4fefec68b123552fa7283da6effedad06/vendor/github.com/google/cel-go/checker/cost.go#L726)
- [Kubernetes CEL's cost estimator](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/aa593eb4fefec68b123552fa7283da6effedad06/staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/cel/library/cost.go#L274)


BUT, [Kubernetes CEL's cost estimator](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/aa593eb4fefec68b123552fa7283da6effedad06/staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/cel/library/cost.go#L274) returns `nil` for most operators which will result `1` for estimation.

So, in this case, the estimation would be `64(=max(string size, list length) * 1`

---

More interestingly, cel-go estimates [`in`(inclusion test) with `O(N)`](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/aa593eb4fefec68b123552fa7283da6effedad06/vendor/github.com/google/cel-go/checker/cost.go#L782-L787) (element size does NOT matter!):

> ```golang
> // evepreace note: args[1] would be list. size(args[1]) would be the number of items in the list
> case overloads.InList:
> 	// If a list is composed entirely of constant values this is O(1), but we don't account for that here.
> 	// We just assume all list containment checks are O(n).
> 	if len(args) == 2 {
> 		return CallEstimate{CostEstimate: c.sizeOrUnknown(args[1]).MultiplyByCostFactor(1).Add(argCostSum())}
> 	}
> ```


And, I think [the current call estimation of `includes`](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/aa593eb4fefec68b123552fa7283da6effedad06/staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/cel/compile.go#L528-L535) aligns to this estimation logic.


```golang
func (e *draCostEstimator) EstimateCallCost(function, overloadID string, target *checker.AstNode, args []checker.AstNode) *checker.CallEstimate {
	if function == "includes" && overloadID == "dra_includes_dyn_dyn" {
		// "<target>.includes(<arg>)" is equivalent with "<arg> in <target>"
		// whose complexity is linear with the size of the target.
		if target != nil {
			targetSizeEstimate := checker.SizeEstimate{Min: 0, Max: resourceapi.ResourceSliceMaxAttributeValuesPerDevice}
			return &checker.CallEstimate{CostEstimate: targetSizeEstimate.MultiplyByCost(checker.CostEstimate{Min: 1, Max: 1})}
		}
	}

	return e.base.EstimateCallCost(function, overloadID, target, args)
}
```

