[staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/kubeletplugin/metadata.go] > IMHO this is problematic and counterintuitive to the effort of standardizing the downward API.

But it's consistent with how APIs get developed in Kubernetes. What makes it a bit worse than API versions in the API server is that there's only on-disk file, whereas in the apiserver clients can read in the version they support.

> Render more than 1 version of metadata in the kubelet plugin directory. May be this can be enabled/disabled by a flag.

This makes sense to me. It emulates the behavior of the API server. We need a file name pattern where consumers can detect that the same information is available in different versions, or put different alternatives into the same file. We could use a JSON stream:
```JSON
{ v1 encoded }
{ v1beta1 encoded }
{ v1alpha1 encoded }
```

Than a decoder can pick the first object that it can decode successfully.

