[pkg/controller/scheduling/podgroupprotection/podgroup_protection_controller.go] I think we can simplify this method especially given what enqueuePodGroupForPod is doing - we're only interested in pod deletions and transitions to terminated state.

```
newPod := getPod(new)
oldPod := getPod(old)
if newPod != nil && isPodTerminated(newPod)
  c.enqueuePodGroupForPod(logger, newPod)
  // An update notification ....
  if oldPod != nil && oldPod.UID != newPod.UID {
    c.enqueuePodGroupForPod(logger, oldPod)
}
if newPod == nil {
  c.enqueuePodGRoupForPod(logger, oldPod)
}
```

Now `enqueuePodGRoupForPod` don't need to check if pod is deleted or terminated because we only pass those that can trigger finalizer removal.

[pkg/controller/scheduling/podgroupprotection/podgroup_protection_controller.go] That would work except that when `newPod` is non-terminated and `oldPod` has a different UID, neither branch is entered. That means the masked deletion of `oldPod` is silently missed. The UID mismatch check needs to happen regardless of whether the new pod is terminated, because the old pod being replaced is effectively deleted. So, it'll be as following:

```go
    newPod := getPod(new)
	oldPod := getPod(old)
	if newPod != nil && isPodTerminated(newPod) {
		c.enqueuePodGroupForPod(logger, newPod)
	}
	// An update notification ...
	if newPod != nil && oldPod != nil && oldPod.UID != newPod.UID {
		c.enqueuePodGroupForPod(logger, oldPod)
	}
	if newPod == nil && oldPod != nil {
		c.enqueuePodGroupForPod(logger, oldPod)
	}
```

I updated it according to your suggestion with the mentioned fix.

