human_review:
[staging/src/k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/apis/meta/v1/types.go] I mentioned this to @Jefftree offline also, but if we want to avoid reinventing grammar, it's possible to use CEL just to parse but in a restrictive way. e.g.

```go
  astResult, issues := env.Parse(exprStr)
    if issues != nil && issues.Err() != nil {
        return issues.Err()
    }
    rootExpr := astResult.Expr()

    call := rootExpr.GetCallExpr()
    if call == nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("expression is not a function call")
    }

    if call.GetFunction() != "shardSelector" {
        return fmt.Errorf("expected 'shardSelector' function call, got '%s'", call.GetFunction())
    }
   ...
```

And skip CEL evaluation entirely, just take the parsed values out of the AST and use them to filter.

Given that we need to support multiple shard ranges, we need *a* grammar, so I agree with @thockin that we might as well use the grammar we're already familiar with.

EDIT:  We could choose to allow `||` also, e..g `shardSelector=shardRange(fieldPath,hexStart,hexEnd)||shardRange(fieldPath,hexStart,hexEnd)`.  The main point is we can allow a strict subset of CEL here and get the benefits of using the CEL parser.

[staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/storage/selection_predicate.go] The signature for `MatchesSharding` already has the check, added it here too just in case. 

```
func (s *SelectionPredicate) MatchesSharding(obj runtime.Object) (bool, error) {
	if !utilfeature.DefaultFeatureGate.Enabled(features.ShardedListAndWatch) {
		return true, nil
	}
        ...
}
```

[staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/server/routes/openapi.go] ```suggestion
				g, ok := m["group"].(string)
				if !ok {
					continue
				}
				v, ok := m["version"].(string)
				if !ok {
					continue
				}
```

[staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/server/routes/openapi.go] only filter if we successfully extracted a group version:

```go
if group, version, ok := groupVersionFromPath(gv) {
  filterScopedGVKs(spec, group, version)
}
```

