```

[pkg/scheduler/apis/config/v1/defaults.go] If we *have* to use noderesourcesfit as the default scorer for Placements, then I agree that MostAllocated is better than LeastAllocated, because it prefers to fill in small "holes" in a placement.

However, I don't think we should use noderesourcesfit.  We should be able to do a good default scoring with something much simpler.

```go
// File: defaultplacementscorer.go

func (dps *DefaultPlacementScorer) ScorePlacement(
   ctx context.Context,
   state fwk.PodGroupCycleState,
   podGroup fwk.PodGroupInfo,
   placement *fwk.PodGroupAssignments
) (int64, *fwk.Status) {
     nodesWithSomePGPod := make([]int, len(podGroupAssignments.Nodes))
	for _, assignment := range podGroupAssignments.ProposedAssignments {
		nodesWithSomePGPod[assignment.GetNode()] = 1
	}
    // Score is ratio of nodes occupied after this placement, time 1000 for fixed point.
    score := 1000 * llen(nodesWithSomePGPod) / len(assignment.Nodes)
	logger := klog.FromContext(ctx)
    // ... rest of debug logging
	return score, nil
}
```

The reason I like the above is:
  - Since it is a net new plugin, there is less risk of disagreement about compatibility problem if we change it later.
  -  It costs less to compute.
  - It does not mislead users into thinking that they can apply their previous experience with scoring plugins, `RequestedToCapacityRatio`, etc, to the problem of TAS scheduling -- when most likely that experience does not directly apply.
  - It works the same as the code in this PR for the common case where workloads either (1) only request accelerator limit equal to all the accelerators on the machine (this is typical) or when they all ask for close to all the cpu and memory on a node (I thinik this is common for non-accelerator TAS workloads).

[pkg/scheduler/apis/config/v1/defaults.go] Just extending a bit on the offline discussion I agree that in 1.36 we do not need any extra configuration options for the placement scoring logic as the existing config for NodeResourcesFit plugin should provide us with all information required to come up with a reasonable default implementation for the plugin placement scoring logic. Moreover by not providing an explicit configuration options now we will not be committing to one very specific implementation which would be very hard to change or remove in the future.

@erictune as commented by @wojtek-t I believe that the proposed simple implementation goes a bit too far when it comes to simplicity by making strong assumptions about the pods and nodes. Overall the behaviour which we want to have is to prefer placements which leave a smaller fraction of resources on the placement to be unused. This should provide a higher chance that subsequent workloads which will come latter may find some placement with enough free resources.

It is true that the implementation proposed in this PR is a bit more demanding from the computational perspective but we already have a similar logic in pod by pod scoring logic so that overhead related to placement scoring should be quite low. There are also some optimizations possible here so as part of beta we can improve the performance if it turns out ot be problematic.

